<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dzungaria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dzungarian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dzungaria is a region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tianshan</a> mountains. It was the heartland of the Dzungar Khanate, a long-standing rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, until the Qing conquest of the 1750s brought it under Chinese rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dzungaria.jpg" alt="Dzungaria" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quigley · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xinjiang_regions_simplified.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dzungaria, also called Northern Xinjiang or Beijiang, is the northern half of Xinjiang and covers about 777,000 square kilometres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> bound it on the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> on the south, and it borders Kazakhstan on the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> on the east. Before the mid-18th century the name could cover a wider area, coterminous with the Oirat-led Dzungar Khanate. Its core is the triangular Dzungarian Basin, with the Gurbantünggüt Desert at its centre. The western corner of the basin is the Dzungarian Gate, a historically important gateway between Dzungaria and the Kazakh Steppe, and the northern corner is the valley of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first inhabitants were Indo-European-speaking peoples such as the Tocharians in prehistory and the Jushi Kingdom in the first millennium BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> sent an explorer west along the northernmost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> trackway, and in the latter half of the sixth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istami">Istämi</a> received Dzungaria as an inheritance from his father. Over the centuries the region was ruled or controlled, wholly or in part, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, among others.</p><p>Travellers going west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> went either north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> through Dzungaria or south of it through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. Trade usually took the south side and migrations the north, because the Tarim leads to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferghana-valley">Ferghana Valley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, while Dzungaria leads only to the open steppe.</p><p>The region takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> people, from the Mongolian zűn gar, meaning left hand, since the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>, stood on the left when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> divided. Dzungar power reached its height in the second half of the 17th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-boshugtu-khan">Galdan Boshugtu Khan</a> repeatedly intervened in the affairs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>, and its widest limit included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a> and Khotan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> destroyed the khanate about 1757–1759. At the time of the Qing conquest Dzungaria was inhabited chiefly by nomadic, steppe-dwelling Tibetan-Buddhist Dzungars. After what is called the Dzungar genocide the Qing repopulated the area with Han and Hui people, and in 1884 they unified Dzungaria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> into one province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main cities are Ürümqi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yining">Yining</a> and Karamay. Wheat, barley, oats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beets are grown, irrigated with melted snow from the mountains, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and horses are raised. The region has deposits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, gold and iron as well as large oil fields. The name has largely disappeared from the map, but survives in the Dzungarian Alatau and in the Djungarian hamster.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dzungaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dzungars were pastoral nomads whose horses, sheep, cattle and camels grazed the grasslands of <b>Dzungaria</b> and the Ili valley, but their rulers worked deliberately to give the state a broader economic base.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They comprised the conquest of <b>Dzungaria</b>, the subjugation of the oasis cities of the Tarim Basin in Eastern Turkestan, campaigns in Burma, Vietnam and Nepal, the suppression of rebellion on Taiwan and the two Jinchuan wars in Sichuan, and they extended the empire to its greatest extent of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Burials of around 200 BCE at Shirenzigou, on the edge of <b>Dzungaria</b>, carry 20–80% Yamnaya-like ancestry.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
